# 1. PROJECT OVERVIEW

I want to build a personal portfolio website that goes beyond the conventional technical portfolio.

I do not want a generic website that simply displays my biography, skills, projects and contact information.

I want a website that tells my story.

The website should communicate the journey of a Microbiology graduate who developed an interest in data science, explored the intersection of biology and technology, began building AI-powered solutions, became involved in technology communities and is now developing an interest in computational biology, bioinformatics and AI research.

It should also communicate my personality, curiosity, values, creativity and willingness to explore ideas across different disciplines.

The experience should feel like exploring the mind and journey of a person, rather than reading a digital CV.

**The central concept is: THE IN-BETWEEN.**

The concept represents the intersections that define my journey:

- Biology and technology.
- Science and artificial intelligence.
- Research and practical problem-solving.
- Local challenges and global possibilities.
- Individual learning and community development.
- Faith, curiosity and data.

The website should make these intersections visible through its storytelling, visual language, content structure and interactions.

# 2. ABOUT THE WEBSITE OWNER

## Professional introduction

My name is Favour Falade.

I am a Microbiology graduate from Ekiti State University, Nigeria, an independent data science practitioner and an interdisciplinary researcher interested in applying artificial intelligence and computational approaches to biological and public health challenges.

My interests include:

- Artificial intelligence and machine learning.
- Computational biology and bioinformatics.
- Antimicrobial resistance.
- Infectious disease research.
- Genomic surveillance.
- AI applications in African healthcare.
- Food microbiology and food systems.
- African language technology.
- Data visualization and analytical storytelling.

I am particularly interested in how technology can help address scientific and societal problems in African contexts.

## My background and journey

I initially wanted to study Medicine but eventually studied Microbiology at Ekiti State University.

My undergraduate experience introduced me to the biological world and strengthened my interest in understanding microorganisms, infectious diseases and their effects on human health.

My experience studying in a resource-constrained academic environment also influenced how I approached learning. Access to some laboratory, computational and bioinformatics resources was limited, so I became increasingly interested in alternative ways to investigate scientific questions and solve problems.

This contributed to my exploration of data science and machine learning.

I began developing technical skills independently and gradually became interested in applying computational methods to biological questions.

My journey has therefore involved moving between disciplines, learning independently, experimenting with ideas and discovering opportunities at the intersection of science and technology.

The website should tell this story honestly and thoughtfully. It should not suggest that I have mastered every field I am interested in.

